    const Section(
      title: '+ Describe the BTB autograft healing timeline and how you would recognize arthrofibrosis and other complications.',
      content: '**BTB Autograft Healing Timeline (Ligamentization Process)**:\n\n**Phase 1 — Avascular Necrosis (Weeks 0-4)**:\n- Graft is essentially "dead tissue" — relies on diffusion for nutrients\n- Central graft necrosis with preservation of outer shell\n- Bone plugs begin incorporating into tunnels\n- **Graft is at its weakest during this period** — must protect\n\n**Phase 2 — Revascularization and Cellular Repopulation (Weeks 4-12)**:\n- New blood vessels infiltrate the graft from the periphery\n- Host cells (fibroblasts) migrate into the graft matrix\n- Graft mechanical strength is at its nadir around 6-8 weeks ("ligamentization valley")\n- **Highest risk period for graft failure if overloaded**\n\n**Phase 3 — Remodeling (Months 3-12)**:\n- Progressive collagen remodeling and maturation\n- Graft gradually approximates native ACL properties\n- Bone plug integration typically solid by 8-12 weeks\n- By 6 months, graft has approximately 50-60% of native ACL strength\n- Full ligamentization may take 12-24+ months\n\n**BTB-Specific Considerations**: Bone-to-bone healing at fixation sites is faster and stronger than soft tissue graft healing (hamstring graft). However, BTB grafts have donor site morbidity — anterior knee pain and kneeling pain in up to 30-40% of patients.\n\n**Recognizing Complications**:\n\n**1. Arthrofibrosis (Stiffness)**:\n- Loss of extension is the most concerning — >5 degrees of extension loss compared to contralateral side at 4-6 weeks is a red flag\n- Flexion <90 degrees at 4-6 weeks should raise concern\n- Risk factors: Pre-operative ROM deficit, early surgery (<3 weeks from injury), excessive inflammation, poor compliance with extension exercises\n- **In this patient**: At 2 weeks, she has 0 degrees extension (excellent) and 72 degrees flexion (on track). Monitor closely at 4 and 6 weeks.\n- **Treatment if detected**: Aggressive extension protocol, low-load long-duration stretching, patellar mobilization. If persistent, lysis of adhesions arthroscopically.\n\n**2. Graft Failure/Re-tear**:\n- Incidence: 5-15% depending on age, sport, and graft type\n- Early failure (<6 months): Usually traumatic or protocol non-compliance\n- Signs: Recurrent instability, giving way episodes, positive Lachman/pivot shift on exam, new effusion\n- Highest risk period for re-tear: First 12 months after return to sport\n\n**3. Infection**:\n- Incidence: 0.5-1%. Presents with increasing pain, warmth, erythema, fever, persistent effusion\n- Requires urgent surgical irrigation and debridement with IV antibiotics\n\n**4. Cyclops Lesion**:\n- Fibrous nodule anterior to the graft causing mechanical block to terminal extension\n- Presents as inability to achieve full extension with a firm endpoint\n- Diagnosed on MRI, treated with arthroscopic excision\n\n**5. Patellar Fracture or Tendon Rupture** (BTB-specific):\n- Rare but devastating. Patellar fracture risk at donor site. Avoid heavy resisted knee extension in early rehabilitation.\n\n**6. Meniscal Repair Failure**:\n- This patient had a lateral meniscus repair — compliance with protected weight-bearing is essential. Deep flexion >90 degrees and squatting are restricted for 4-6 weeks to protect the repair.',
