ically 3-4 months): No effusion, full ROM, quad strength >70%, single-leg squat with good mechanics, no pain with hopping.\n- **Running progression**: Walk-jog intervals on flat surface, progress distance and speed over 6-8 weeks. Forward running only initially.\n- **Exercises**: Full-depth squats, single-leg exercises (Bulgarian split squats, single-leg deadlifts), plyometric preparation (double-leg jump landing drills), lateral band walks, perturbation training on wobble board.\n- **Neuromuscular training**: ACL prevention program principles (landing mechanics, deceleration training, hip-dominant movement patterns). This reduces re-injury risk and addresses the biomechanical factors that contributed to the original injury.\n\n**Phase 4 — Sport-Specific Training (Months 6-9)**:\n- **Goals**: Quad and hamstring strength >90% of contralateral, pass functional tests, progress to cutting and pivoting, psychological readiness.\n- **Exercises**: Agility drills (ladder, cones), cutting progressions (45-degree cuts, then 90-degree cuts, then reactive cutting), plyometrics (single-leg hops, box jumps, depth jumps), sport-specific drills (soccer — dribbling, passing, shooting progressions without contact).\n- **On-field progression**: Individual drills, then team drills without contact, then controlled scrimmaging, then full contact practice.\n\n**Phase 5 — Return to Competition (Months 9-12+)**:\n- Must meet ALL return-to-sport criteria before clearance (see Domain D)\n- Gradual reintroduction to full competition\n- Maintenance of neuromuscular prevention program indefinitely',
