      content: '**Objective Return-to-Sport Criteria** (ALL must be met):\n\n**1. Time-Based Minimum**:\n- Minimum **9 months** post-surgery for BTB autograft ACL reconstruction. Many sports medicine specialists now recommend 12+ months based on evidence that every month of delay in return to sport (up to 9 months) reduces re-injury risk by 51%.\n- With concomitant meniscal repair, 9-12 months is appropriate.\n\n**2. Strength Testing**:\n- **Quadriceps Limb Symmetry Index (LSI) >90%**: Isokinetic testing at 60 degrees/sec and 180 degrees/sec. Both concentric quadriceps and hamstring strength must be >90% of the contralateral limb.\n- **Hamstring-to-quadriceps ratio >60%** on the operative leg.\n- **Hip abductor and external rotator strength**: Symmetric bilaterally. Deficit increases dynamic valgus risk.\n\n**3. Functional Hop Testing Battery (LSI >90% on all)**:\n- **Single-leg hop for distance**: Single maximum hop, compare limb symmetry\n- **Triple hop for distance**: Three consecutive single-leg hops\n- **Crossover hop for distance**: Three hops crossing over a line\n- **Timed 6-meter hop**: Single-leg hopping for speed\n- All four tests must achieve >90% LSI to pass\n\n**4. Movement Quality Assessment**:\n- **Single-leg squat test**: Observe for dynamic knee valgus, trunk lean, Trendelenburg\n- **Drop vertical jump (DVJ)**: Video analysis for knee valgus angle on landing. Acceptable landing mechanics without medial knee collapse.\n- **Landing Error Scoring System (LESS)**: Standardized assessment of jump-landing biomechanics\n- **Y-Balance Test**: Composite reach score >95% of contralateral limb\n\n**5. Sport-Specific Functional Testing**:\n- Successful completion of progressive agility and cutting drills without pain, effusion, or apprehension\n- Sport-specific drills at game speed (for soccer: sprinting, cutting, heading, kicking)\n- Full practice participation for at least 2-4 weeks without setback\n\n**6. Psychological Readiness** (Often overlooked but critical):\n- **ACL-RSI Scale**: Score >56 out of 100 associated with successful return. Her initial score was 28 — this needs significant improvement.\n- **Tampa Scale of Kinesiophobia**: Assess fear of movement and re-injury.\n- Subjective confidence: Patient should self-report >80% confidence in the knee.\n- Components assessed: Confidence in knee function, absence of fear during sport-specific activities, re-injury anxiety level, readiness to accept risk.\n\n**For this patient at 7 months**: She is NOT ready. Minimum time criteria not met (9 months). Must complete all functional testing. Her low ACL-RSI score suggests she needs sports psychology support before return is appropriate. It would be premature and unsafe to clear her at this time, regardless of coach pressure.',
